WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.290
Part 3.

2
00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:11.370
You will hear two education students discussing an assignment on teaching handwriting to children.

3
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:17.130
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 24.

4
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.940
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 24.

5
00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.270
<v STUDENT 1> How are you getting on with the assignment on handwriting?

6
00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.150
<v STUDENT 2> Not too bad.

7
00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:01.190
<v STUDENT 2> You know, I hadn't realised that children benefit in so many ways from learning to write.

8
00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:12.090
<v STUDENT 2> It's such an important skill and yet most people think handwriting is less important than in the past because people hardly ever write by hand these days.

9
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:20.890
<v STUDENT 1> Yes, and all the evidence suggests children should learn to write by hand before they learn to type, not least because it helps their memory.

10
00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:21.890
<v STUDENT 2> That's right.

11
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.310
<v STUDENT 2> The physical act of writing helps children to remember letters.

12
00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.850
<v STUDENT 2> That seems pretty obvious when you think about it.

13
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.910
<v STUDENT 1> What's less obvious is how it helps develop their concentration.

14
00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:37.550
<v STUDENT 1> They have to sit still and focus on one thing.

15
00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.930
<v STUDENT 2> Yeah, that aspect of handwriting had never occurred to me before.

16
00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.090
<v STUDENT 1> Same here.

17
00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:48.010
<v STUDENT 1> I'm not sure I understand how it improves children's imagination though.

18
00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:57.410
<v STUDENT 2> Well, there was that study which showed that primary age children generated more ideas when they were writing by hand than using a keyboard.

19
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.110
<v STUDENT 2> I would have guessed that would be the case.

20
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:01.370
<v STUDENT 1> Hmm, yeah.

21
00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:06.030
<v STUDENT 1> I'd never associated spatial awareness with handwriting either.

22
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:13.130
<v STUDENT 1> I thought spatial awareness was more to do with knowing where you are in relation to objects or other people.

23
00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.710
<v STUDENT 2> I thought that too.

24
00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:21.450
<v STUDENT 2> But good spatial awareness is essential for writing because you have to space words correctly.

25
00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:27.290
<v STUDENT 2> It's not just fine motor skills that improve through writing as I'd always assumed.

26
00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:34.330
<v STUDENT 1> Handwriting is so much harder for children with dyspraxia who have problems coordinating movement.

27
00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:38.230
<v STUDENT 1> It's good there are lots of things you can do in the classroom to help them.

28
00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:41.530
<v STUDENT 1> They need so much more support with letter formation.

29
00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.510
<v STUDENT 1> You need to play lots of games to help them distinguish letter shapes.

30
00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:47.670
<v STUDENT 1> It takes a lot of patience.

31
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:48.230
<v STUDENT 2> Yeah.

32
00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:53.870
<v STUDENT 2> I like the idea of using one of those pens that lights up if you press too hard.

33
00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.530
<v STUDENT 2> That seems like a really simple solution.

34
00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:57.850
<v STUDENT 1> Yes, absolutely.

35
00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:03.710
<v STUDENT 1> I'm not sure there's much you can do about children with dyspraxia writing very slowly.

36
00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:11.830
<v STUDENT 1> It's more important to focus on accuracy and as they get more confident, I think they eventually speed up.

37
00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.170
<v STUDENT 2> One quite simple thing you can do is to use grid paper.

38
00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:22.110
<v STUDENT 2> So they write each letter in a box and that trains them to space the letters correctly.

39
00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:22.730
<v STUDENT 1> Indeed.

40
00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:27.570
<v STUDENT 1> That's more important for legibility than trying to get them to write in a straight line.

41
00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:37.440
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 25 to 30.

42
00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:31.740
Now listen and answer questions 25 to 30.

43
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:44.000
<v STUDENT 1> For some children, it might be better to teach them to write on a laptop rather than by hand, like children with dyslexia.

44
00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:48.520
<v STUDENT 1> They often really struggle with handwriting and some just give up.

45
00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:49.080
<v STUDENT 2> Yeah.

46
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.860
<v STUDENT 2> It's not as frustrating for them if they get things wrong.

47
00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.200
<v STUDENT 2> On a keyboard, they can be more willing to have a go.

48
00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:00.740
<v STUDENT 2> But I read that developing fluency isn't any faster.

49
00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:01.740
<v STUDENT 1> That's right.

50
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:09.720
<v STUDENT 1> Did you read that article on the benefits of teaching print rather than cursive handwriting, where the letters are joined up?

51
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.280
<v STUDENT 2> Yes.

52
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:18.100
<v STUDENT 2> Well, in the past, cursive writing was certainly considered more stylish and educated, but not anymore.

53
00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:30.820
<v STUDENT 2> Teachers' attitudes have changed because it's been proved that cursive is more difficult to learn, especially for children with learning difficulties who find joining up letters really challenging.

54
00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:31.920
<v STUDENT 1> I agree.

55
00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:37.040
<v STUDENT 1> I was always worried that my poor handwriting affected my exam results.

56
00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.900
<v STUDENT 1> And now research shows that I was right to worry.

57
00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:47.800
<v STUDENT 1> I'm sure a lot of students think it's unfair that they're being judged on their handwriting, not just their knowledge.

58
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.060
<v STUDENT 2> Marks are definitely affected if examiners can't read the script.

59
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.760
<v STUDENT 2> That's why it's always been so important to teach children to write legibly.

60
00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.080
<v STUDENT 2> Do you think the role of handwriting will change in the future?

61
00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:04.240
<v STUDENT 1> I can't see that changing much.

62
00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.840
<v STUDENT 1> Touch typing still isn't taught in most schools, which is a shame.

63
00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:16.360
<v STUDENT 1> But maybe that won't be necessary in the future because people will also be able to write by hand on digital devices.

64
00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.880
<v STUDENT 1> Anyway, teachers understand the value of handwriting.

65
00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:23.040
<v STUDENT 1> It's a basic life skill.

66
00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:23.640
<v STUDENT 2> True.

67
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:31.460
<v STUDENT 2> However, the fact is that people are writing by hand less and less and relying on digital devices.

68
00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:33.660
<v STUDENT 2> That does cause some problems.

69
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.080
<v STUDENT 1> You mean like note-taking?

70
00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:38.380
<v STUDENT 1> There are lots of apps for that.

71
00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.540
<v STUDENT 2> And for reading historical documents, apparently.

72
00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:48.480
<v STUDENT 2> But my mum is shocked by my awful spelling and the fact that my punctuation is really inconsistent.

73
00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:51.520
<v STUDENT 2> I think you can put that down to lack of practice.

74
00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.360
<v STUDENT 1> I expect so.

75
00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.600
<v STUDENT 1> Personally, I miss writing by hand.

76
00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.940
<v STUDENT 1> I hardly ever write anything now.

77
00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:04.780
<v STUDENT 1> I remember my grandparents had such beautiful handwriting and it was so individual.

78
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.260
<v STUDENT 1> Nobody I know would be able to identify my handwriting now.

79
00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:10.320
<v STUDENT 1> It's a shame.

80
00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:11.220
<v STUDENT 2> I know.

81
00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:12.800
<v STUDENT 2> I feel the same way.

82
00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:17.820
<v STUDENT 2> I used to write a diary by hand and now I do that digitally.

83
00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.260
<v STUDENT 2> It just seems less effort to do it that way.

84
00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.140
<v STUDENT 2> So, it's not just a problem.

85
00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:29.650
That is the end of Part 3.

86
00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:34.510
You now have 30 seconds to check your answers to Part 3.
